Suppose $C$ is a computable set such that $\Th{Q} \subseteq C$ and
$\Th{\bar Q} \subseteq \Complement{C}$. Let $R(x,y)$ be the relation
\begin{quote}
$x$ codes a formula $!D(u)$ and $!D(\num y)$ is in $C$.
\end{quote}
We will show that $R(x,y)$ is a universal computable relation, yielding
a contradiction.

Suppose $S(y)$ is computable, represented by $!D_S(u)$ in $\Th{Q}$. Then
\begin{eqnarray*}
S(\num n) & \lif & \Th{Q} \Proves !D_S(\num n) \\
& \lif & !D_S(\num n) \in C
\end{eqnarray*}
and 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lnot S(\num n) & \lif & \Th{Q} \Proves \lnot !D_S(\num n) \\
& \lif & !D_S(\num n) \in \Th{\bar Q} \\
& \lif & !D_S(\num n) \not\in C
\end{eqnarray*}
So $S(y)$ is equivalent to $R(\#(!D_S(\num u)),y)$.
